power for the first time, by cracking down on the \\\"DJI front companies\\\" suspected of sneaking the Chinese company's tech past its foreign drone ban . Two Fridays ago, the FCC had already proposed a $25,000 fine on eight of those \\\"front companies,\\\" including the ones behind the Skyrover drones and Xtra cameras . But now, the FCC is proposing a lot more than a slap on the wrist - it's planning to ban those same companies from … Read the full story at The Verge.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://seekingalpha.com/tag/etf-portfolio-strategy.xml\",\"feedTitle\":\"ETFs & Portfolio Strategy Articles on Seeking Alpha\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=seekingalpha.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Investing\",\"title\":\"EWY Vs. EWH: Michael Burry Has This Backwards\",\"link\":\"https://seekingalpha.com/article/4923576-ewy-vs-ewh-michael-burry-has-this-backwards?source=feed_tag_etf_portfolio_strategy\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fseekingalpha.com%2Farticle%2F4923576-ewy-vs-ewh-michael-burry-has-this-backwards%3Fsource%3Dfeed_tag_etf_portfolio_strategy&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dseekingalpha.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-20T22:16:29.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"American’s New Flagship Suites Are Coming to Another Dreamliner — Here’s What Travelers Need To Know\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/news/news-american-flagship-suites-787-8/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/AA-Flagship-Suite-Business-Class-1L-My-Seat.jpg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-20T22:16:15.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"If you’ve been hoping to experience American Airlines’ new Flagship Suites without hunting down one of the carrier’s newest Boeing 787-9s, there’s some good news. American has confirmed that its Boeing 787-8 Dreamliners will also receive the airline’s latest business class product, bringing its most modern premium cabin to even more long-haul flights. While there’s no word yet on when the retrofits will begin, it’s a welcome sign that American is committed to rolling out its new Flagship experience across more of its international fleet. Here’s what we know so far. American’s Boeing 787-8 Is Getting Flagship Suites American Airlines has confirmed that its fleet of 37 Boeing 787-8 Dreamliners will be the next aircraft to receive the carrier’s new Flagship Suites (Source: CNBC ). American first introduced the premium cabin on select newly delivered Boeing 787-9 Dreamliners in June 2025, where each suite features a sliding privacy door, wireless charging, additional storage, and a swankier design than the airline’s previous-generation business class product . American’s Boeing 787-8 Dreamliners are next in line to receive the airline’s new Flagship Suites. Image Credit: Juan Ruiz While the 787-8 already offers lie-flat seats with direct aisle access, the retrofit will bring a noticeably more premium onboard experience . American is also expected to refresh the rest of the cabin, giving these Dreamliners an updated look that better matches the airline’s newer 787-9 wide-body aircraft. The Boeing 787-8 cabin refresh extends beyond business class. Image Credit: Juan Ruiz Right now, American’s newest Flagship Suites are available on just a handful of routes. Travelers can currently find the new cabins on flights from Chicago (ORD) to London (LHR) and Los Angeles (LAX), Philadelphia (PHL) to London (LHR) and Zurich (ZRH), and Dallas Fort Worth (DFW) to Brisbane (BNE). There’s still no timeline for when the first retrofitted 787-8 will enter service. But as American adds more Boeing 787-9s and eventually upgrades its fleet of 787-8s, travelers should have many more opportunities to book Flagship Suites on the airline’s long-haul routes. Hot Tip: We recently reviewed American’s new Flagship Suites on the Boeing 787-9 and found them to be a major upgrade over the airline’s older business class seats, especially those on many Boeing 777s. Final Thoughts As someone who’s flown American’s new Flagship Suites, it’s exciting to see the airline expand the product beyond just a handful of aircraft. The more Boeing 787-8s that receive the new cabins, the more travelers will have access to what is easily American’s best business class experience — and that’s a win for anyone flying the nation’s largest airline.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"Last Chance for Increased Welcome Offers on Hilton Amex Cards [Up to 175K Points]\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/news/new-welcome-bonuses-hilton-american-express-cards/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/3-Hilton-Credit-Cards-in-a-Beige-Wallet-Upgraded-Points-LLC.jpg.jpg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-20T21:49:42.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Right now, you can score elevated welcome bonus offers across the range of Hilton’s co-branded credit cards issued by American Express — but these offers are set to end soon, on July 29, 2026. Let’s take a look at what you can earn with each card and help you determine which option is best. Hilton Honors Cards Limited-Time Offers Ending Soon If you’re in the market for more Hilton Honors points, you’ll want to check out these increased welcome offers, all of which are slated to end soon on July 29, 2026 . Hilton Honors American Express Aspire Card The Hilton Aspire card is the top-tier offering in Amex’s lineup of Hilton co-branded credit cards. Now through July 29, 2026, you can earn 175,000 bonus Hilton Honors points after spending $6,000 on purchases in the first 6 months of card membership. The card has an annual fee of $550 ( rates & fees ). As the top option in the lineup, the Hilton Aspire card offers a powerful earning structure and a laundry list of meaningful perks and benefits: Earn 14x points on Hilton purchases. Earn 7x points on flights booked directly through the airline or AmexTravel.com, and on car rentals booked directly through select agencies. Earn 7x points at U.S. restaurants (including takeout and delivery). Earn 3x points on all other purchases. Receive a free night certificate each yea r on your card anniversary. Receive up to $400 in resort credits (up to $200 in credits semiannually) when you stay at participating resorts. Receive complimentary top-tier Hilton Honors Diamond elite status . Hilton Honors American Express Surpass® Card The midrange option in Hilton’s lineup of co-branded cards, the Hilton Surpass card is offering 130,000 bonus Hilton Honors points after spending $3,000 on purchases within the first 6 months of card membership. The card has a $0 introductory annual fee for the first year, then $150 annual fee ( rates & fees ). This offer ends on July 29, 2026. The Hilton Surpass card offers a solid blend of earning and perks for Hilton stays: Earn 12x points on Hilton purchases. Earn 6x points at U.S. restaurants, supermarkets, and gas stations. Earn 4x points on online U.S. retail purchases. Earn 3x points on all other purchases. Earn a free night certificate after spending $15,000 in eligible purchases on your card. Get up to $50 in statement credits each quarter for purchases made directly at a Hilton property, up to $200 annually. Receive complimentary Hilton Honors Gold elite status. Spend $40,000 on eligible purchases on your card within a calendar year and receive top-tier Hilton Honors Diamond elite status through the end of the following year. Hilton Honors American Express Card The Hilton Honors card is the entry-level Hilton card from American Express. Now through July 29, 2026, you can earn 100,000 Hilton Honors bonus points and a $100 statement credit after spending $2,000 on purchases in the first 6 months of card membership. The card has a $0 annual fee ( rates & fees ). This card doesn’t include many premium benefits, but it does offer a good way to earn Hilton Honors points : Earn 7x points on Hilton purchases. Earn 5x points at U.S. restaurants (including takeout and delivery). Earn 5x points at U.S. supermarkets. Earn 5x points at U.S. gas stations. Earn 3x points on all other purchases. Receive complimentary Hilton Honors Silver elite status . The Hilton Honors American Express Business Card Also available through July 29, 2026, Hilton’s only business card is offering 130,000 bonus Hilton Honors points after you spend $8,000 in purchases within 6 months of card membership. Also, as part of the offer, the card has an annual fee of $0 introductory annual fee for the first year, then $195 ( rates & fees ). The Hilton Business Amex card offers business owners an easy way to earn lots of Hilton Honors points: Earn 12x points on Hilton purchases. Earn 5x points on other purchases made using the Hilton Business Amex card on the first $100,000 in purchases each calendar year. Earn 3x points thereafter. Plus, this card comes with complimentary Hilton Honors Gold status and a free night certificate after spending $15,000 on purchases in a calendar year. You can book a stay at the Waldorf Astoria Las Vegas with Hilton Honors points or a free night certificate. Image Credit: Nick Ellis Hot Tip: Need some inspiration? Read about all the best ways to redeem Hilton Honors points for maximum value . Final Thoughts Hilton and American Express are offering elevated welcome offers across their portfolio of co-branded credit cards. If you’ve been wanting to increase your Hilton points balance, now’s a great time to add one of these cards to your wallet. Remember to apply soon before these offers end on July 29, 2026, if you’re interested!\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://www.theverge.com/rss/index.xml\",\"feedTitle\":\"The Verge\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.theverge.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Tech\",\"title\":\"The Odyssey turned me into an IMAX believer\",\"link\":\"https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/968337/the-odyssey-imax-screenings-christopher-nolan\",\"image\":\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/ODY_AdvSlvrSoldiers1Sht1_RGB_6SM.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-20T21:47:37.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"After seeing Christopher Nolan's The Odyssey for the first time early last week, I came away impressed, but somewhat conflicted about two of the film's more fantastical set pieces. While those scenes were beautifully crafted and tremendously acted, there was something a little bit off about the way they hit my eye. I spent days wondering what, exactly, wasn't quite working for me during that first theatrical viewing, and I thought that might have just been splitting hairs. But it wasn't until I saw the movie again over the weekend - this time in IMAX - that the issue became crystal clear. Though I'd heard all of the buzz about The Odyssey b … Read the full story at The Verge.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/rss\",\"feedTitle\":\"Hacker News\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=news.ycombinator.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Tech\",\"title\":\"Donation Controversy\",\"link\":\"https://mullvad.net/en/blog/donation-controversy\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmullvad.net%2Fen%2Fblog%2Fdonation-controversy&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dnews.ycombinator.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-20T21:43:07.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Comments\",\"replyCou