unt\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://www.theverge.com/rss/index.xml\",\"feedTitle\":\"The Verge\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.theverge.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Tech\",\"title\":\"This unpronounceable series of glyphs is an incredible side project from Kieran Hebden (aka Four Tet)\",\"link\":\"https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/967696/four-tet-wingdings-album-review\",\"image\":\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/four-tet-wingdings.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-19T21:02:45.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Sure is a lot of porcelein in there. | Image: Kieran Hebden Just why? ʅ͡͡͡͡͡͡͡͡͡͡͡(̸̢̛̼̞̭͋ͅ)̸͚̰͛̔̾̀̿͒͂:̴͓̞̑̌̂̆̊͋̀:̸͎̟̯̂̓̌　҉　　　　　͡　͞　͞　͞　҉●&emsp;࿀&emsp;●&emsp;&emsp;࿀&emsp;&emsp;●　　　҉⃝ 　　　⃝͢ ͞　　　͘　͞⃝̕ ͢　　　　̛　⃝ 　　　̸　̡　　͢⃝̧ 　͡　͡　　̀　̧　̢⃝͜ 　 　҉　　͞　͞　⃝͞ 　͞　͡⃝ &emsp;⃝҉҈҉҈҉҈҉҈҉҈҉ :̶̢͙͙͕̠̩͆(̷̮͍͚̫͚͂̍)̵̳̗̊(　̟̞̝̜̙̘̗̖҉̵̴̨̧̢̡̼̻̺̹̳̲̱̰̯̮̭̬̫̪̩̦̥ What am I supposed to do with this? It's un-Googleable. Unpronounceable. It doesn't even render the same way on various platforms. It looks one way on YouTube Music. Another on Apple Music. And yet another on Bandcamp. Even the artist name ⣎⡇ꉺლ༽இ&bull;̛)ྀ◞ ༎ຶ ༽ৣৢ؞ৢ؞ؖ ꉺლ is an unpronounceable series of glyphs that would make Prince shake his head. So, from here on out, I'm just going to refer to ⣎⡇ꉺლ༽இ&bull;̛)ྀ◞ ༎ຶ … Read the full story at The Verge.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"[Award Alert] Air France Business Class Availability From Paris From 45K Miles\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/news/award-alert-paris-us-business-class/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/PXL_20240909_095909671.jpg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-19T21:01:06.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Those planning a European getaway over the next few months will love the award availability we’re seeing on Air France flights from Paris, bookable via Air France-KLM Flying Blue. With prices as low as 45,000 miles per person , this is a deal you should jump on, even if you weren’t already planning a trip to France. Here’s everything you need to know and how to book for yourself. Award Alert — The Details For these flights, you’ll fly with Air France and book through the Flying Blue program. Thanks to Roame , we’re seeing excellent availability across several months from Paris to New York , with seats starting at just 45,000 miles one-way in business class. If you are already planning to be in Europe and need a flight back to the U.S., this is a fantastic opportunity. Class:  Business Route: Paris (CDG) to New York (JFK) Cost: From 45,000 points plus $605 in taxes and fees Travel dates: July 2026 to September 2026 Seats: Up to 9 in business class, up to 9 in economy You can use a tool like Roame to check multiple rates and a range of dates all at once. That way, you can do some research on ideal dates and award space before logging in to your Air France or KLM account to make a reservation. Image Credit: Roame Flying from Paris to JFK in Air France business class for 45,000 miles is a great deal . Even with the higher taxes and fees, these tickets typically sell for over $5,300 on the one-way route, so you’re still redeeming your miles for over 10 cents each! Image Credit: Roame What’s most exciting about this award alert is that we’re seeing up to 9 business class seats available on some dates , a rare find for those planning to travel with family or friends. If you’d rather save your points and cash, economy deals can be incredible bargains. We’ve spotted economy seats from as low as 25,000 points one-way from Paris to New York, depending on the travel dates. Hot Tip: Planning a summer trip to Paris? Be sure to check out these exclusive visitor benefits at Printemps Haussmann , the city’s most iconic department store. Visitors can save up to 10% on purchases and get a tax refund of up to 16% through August 31, 2026, by presenting this exclusive invitation to the Printemps Paris reception desk. How To Book While there are a couple of different ways to book these flights, the best price and availability are via Flying Blue. Flying Blue is a transfer partner for virtually every credit card program, and Citi ThankYou Rewards offers a 20% bonus on transfers through August 22. Other options for earning Flying Blue miles include dining out, shopping online, renting cars, and more. Final Thoughts The ability to book a one-way Air France business class award from Paris starting at just 45,000 miles for flights through September is excellent value . If you’ve got a European getaway planned, this lie-flat seat could be your ride home.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"How To Book a Mexico City Weekend Using the 100K Chase Sapphire Preferred Bonus\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/news/sapphire-preferred-100k-bonus-mexico-city-weekend/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/02/Mexico-City-Zocalo-Plaza-Cathedral-Flag.jpg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-19T21:00:00.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"The Chase Sapphire Preferred® Card is offering its highest-ever welcome bonus right now, though it’s set to end soon: Earn 75,000 100,000 points after spending $5,000 on purchases in the first 3 months. If you want to see what that number actually buys in the real world, Mexico City makes a strong case. Most U.S. travelers sleep on CDMX (as it’s known to locals) as a points destination, but they shouldn’t. It’s not far from most U.S. hubs — short enough to keep flight costs manageable and to make a long weekend feel like a trip rather than a slog. 3 nights in Mexico City gives you enough time to eat in Roma Norte, wander Polanco, and still hit a mezcal bar in Condesa before your flight home. And with 100,000 Chase Ultimate Rewards points, you can cover both flights and a genuinely good hotel. Here’s how the math works across 3 origin cities to see these redemptions in practice. Chase Sapphire Preferred Card’s 100,000-Point Welcome Offer New applicants for the Chase Sapphire Preferred card can earn 100,000 Ultimate Rewards points after spending $5,000 on purchases in the first 3 months. To be eligible, applicants should have good or excellent credit, have opened fewer than 5 credit cards across all issuers in the past 24 months (known as the Chase 5/24 rule ), and not received a welcome offer bonus on this card in the past. » Related: The Chase Sapphire Preferred Card’s 100,000-Point Welcome Offer Is Ending Soon How Chase Points Work for This Trip Chase Ultimate Rewards points transfer 1:1 to most airline and hotel partners. The one exception is that points from the Chase Sapphire Preferred card now transfer to World of Hyatt at a 4:3 ratio for cardholders who applied on or after June 15, 2026. In other words, every 4 Ultimate Rewards points becomes 3 World of Hyatt points. This impacts the hotel math below, so it’s worth keeping in mind before you start transferring. For flights, the strongest options are United MileagePlus and British Airways Club , both of which transfer 1:1 from Chase. Flights to Mexico City With Chase Points United Airlines flies to Mexico City’s Benito Juárez International (MEX) from multiple U.S. hubs. United uses dynamic pricing (meaning price depends on demand), but Saver-level economy awards to Mexico remain among the cheapest in the program and are worth targeting. Redeeming United miles should price as follows: From Houston (IAH) : Round-trip economy Saver awards run as few as 20,000 miles From Chicago (ORD) or Los Angeles (LAX) : Expect 25,000–30,000 miles round-trip From Newark (EWR) : Plan for roughly 35,000 miles round-trip Search for Saver availability directly on united.com . Saver fares (the cheapest tickets) are clearly marked and easy to spot. Redeeming British Airways Avios is a compelling alternative, especially if American Airlines has nonstop service from your home airport. British Airways prices its partner awards by distance rather than dynamically, which works in your favor on short- to medium-distance flights like this one. For most cities within 2,000 miles of MEX , including Chicago (ORD) , Los Angeles (LAX), Dallas (DFW) , and Miami (MIA) , flights fall into Zone 3 pricing: 36,000 Avios round-trip (18,000 each way) From New York (JFK) : The distance pushes into Zone 4 at 40,000 Avios round-trip (20,000 each way) Taxes and carrier fees on American Airlines bookings through British Airways typically run under $25 round-trip, so using Avios covers nearly the entire cost. Hot Tip: British Airways prices each flight segment separately, so a nonstop is always cheaper than a 1-stop. Where To Stay in Mexico City With Chase Points World of Hyatt has the strongest hotel options in Mexico City , even accounting for the new 4:3 transfer ratio. The Andaz Mexico City Condesa is my top pick. It’s in Condesa — one of the best neighborhoods to base yourself in — and the property earns the Andaz design-forward reputation. It’s a Category 4 Hyatt, and the new 5-tier award pricing means you pay 12,000 to 25,000 points per night — with 15,000 or 20,000 points being the most common nightly rates. With the 4:3 ratio from the recent Chase Sapphire Preferred card refresh, those numbers require transferring 20,000 to 27,000 Ultimate Rewards points. Booking 3 nights at the 15,000-point rate requires transferring 60,000 points. Cash rates at this hotel regularly run $250 to $350 per night, so the value is real. Cabuya rooftop bar at the Andaz Mexico City Condesa. Image Credit: Ryan Smith The Hyatt Regency Mexico City in Polanco is the more budget-conscious Hyatt. It’s a Category 3 property, and while it’s more of a business hotel, it’s well located and well run. Award nights range from 8,000 to 20,000 Hyatt points, with 12,000- and 15,000-point nights being among the most common. Under the new 4:3 transfer ratio, that works out to about 16,000 or 20,000 Ultimate Rewards points per night, meaning a 3-night stay generally requires 48,000 to 60,000 Ultimate Rewards points . Wyndham Rewards uses fixed-tier pricing, which makes the math simple. Most Mexico City properties, including the BelAir Unique , a Wyndham Hotel, run 15,000 Wyndham points per night. Since Wyndham transfers 1:1 from Chase, a 3-night stay would cost 45,000 Ultimate Rewards points. It’s the budget-friendly tier of the bunch. Hot Tip: The 4:3 Hyatt transfer ratio only affects Chase Sapphire Preferred cardholders who applied on or after June 15, 2026. If you applied before that date, you can still transfer to Hyatt at 1:1 through October 1, 2026. 3 Ways To Use 100K Points for a Mexico City Trip Here are 3 combinations that each land under 100,000 Ultimate Rewards points. From Houston Transfer 20,000 points to United MileagePlus for round-trip economy from IAH. Transfer 48,000 points to Hyatt for 3 lowest-rate nights at the Andaz Mexico City Condesa (the 4:3 ratio turns 48,000 Ultimate Rewards points into 36,000 Hyatt points). Total: 68,000 points. You’d still have 32,000 points left over, which is enough to bring a friend or loved one on your United flight (yes, round-trip). From Chicago or Los Angeles Transfer 36,000 points to British Airways for a round-trip economy seat on American Airlines from ORD or LAX. Transfer 45,000 points to Wyndham for 3 hotel nights. Total: 81,000 Ultimate Rewards points. The shorter per-night cost at the Wyndham offsets the higher flight cost from farther-flung cities. From New York Transfer 40,000 points to British Airways for round-trip economy seats on American Airlines from JFK. Transfer 48,000 points to Hyatt for 3 lowest-rate nights at the Andaz Mexico City. Total: 88,000 Ultimate Rewards points. Even from the East Coast — the most expensive flight scenario here — you land 12,000 points under what you earned from your credit card’s welcome offer . » Related: You Don’t Want To Miss These 17 Sites on Your Next Trip to Mexico Final Thoughts The 100,000-point welcome offer on the Chase Sapphire Preferred card is rare. It has only surfaced a handful of times in the card’s history, and it’s enough to fund a complete Mexico City long weekend — including flights and hotel — from nearly anywhere in the country. Chase has officially informed us that this 100,000-point bonus offer is ending soon, so there’s not much time left to take advantage. Mexico City is worth more than a weekend, but it’s an excellent place to find out whether you want to go back for longer.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://search.cnbc.com/rs/search/combinedcms/view.xml?partnerId=wrss01&id=100003114\",\"feedTitle\":\"US Top News and Analysis\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=cnbc.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"Justice Department activates untested court for 'alien terrorist' deportations\",\"link\":\"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/19/justice-department-activates-court-for-alien-terrorist-deportations.html\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cnbc.com%2F2026%2F07%2F19%2Fjustice-department-activates-court-for-alien-terrorist-deportations.html&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dcnbc.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-19T20:34:35.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Judge James Boasberg has suggested the Alien Terrorist Removal Court would be the natural forum for considering deportation on national security grounds.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://voz.vn/f/chuyen-tro-linh-tinh-tm.17/index.rss\",\"feedTitle\":\