2018 rule that generally allowed a tax deduction for theft losses and would add other relief for fraud victims.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"6 of the Best Michelin Key Hotels You Can Book With Hilton, IHG, and Marriott Points\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/news/best-michelin-key-hotels-with-points/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/roku-kyoto-entrance-2.jpg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T13:30:00.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Many people are familiar with the Michelin Guide’s approach to restaurants, often considered the gold standard for top-tier dining experiences, but Michelin extends recognition to luxury hospitality experiences worldwide. Select hotels that have received a Michelin Key can be booked with hotel points, offering a luxury hotel experience without breaking the bank. Here’s a look at some of the best Michelin Key hotels you can book using your Hilton, IHG, and Marriott points. How Michelin Awards Hotel Keys Michelin evaluates 5 important criteria during their  hotel assessments : The hotel is an open door to the destination Excellence in architecture and interior design Quality and consistency of service, comfort, and maintenance Consistency between the experience and price Individuality, reflecting personality and authenticity This process is similar to how Michelin evaluates restaurants. Hotel award designations follow a 3-tier structure, with notable properties receiving 1 to 3 Michelin Keys. 1 Key : A very special stay with standout character and thoughtful service 2 Keys:  An exceptional stay with a unique design and a consistently memorable experience 3 Keys : An extraordinary stay and among the most remarkable hotels in the world Benefits of Booking With Marriott, IHG, and Hilton Points Booking an award stay at a Michelin Key hotel lets you enjoy a luxury stay while avoiding steep cash rates. Better yet, redeeming points through Marriott, IHG, or Hilton points allows you to use elite status benefits. Depending on your status, you may receive added perks during your stay, such as: Complimentary room upgrades Free breakfast Early check-in and late checkout Bonus points Welcome amenities Hot Tip: See the top Michelin Key hotels bookable with Hyatt points , along with Chase’s The Edit and Amex Fine Hotels and Resorts . Best Credit Cards for Earning Hilton, IHG, and Marriott Points While there are several excellent Marriott , IHG , and Hilton card options, here is a quick overview of the best overall card for each brand. Marriott Bonvoy Brilliant® American Express® Card The Marriott Bonvoy Brilliant card is the best card option for those who frequently stay at Marriott hotels. It has several premium benefits, including: Earning points: 6x points on Marriott stays, 3x points on flights and restaurants, and 2x points on all other purchases. Automatic Marriott Bonvoy Platinum Elite status : Complimentary room upgrades upon availability (including standard suites), 50% bonus points, welcome gift choice (including complimentary breakfast), and 4 p.m. late checkout (based on availability). Annual free night award: Redeemable at any Marriott property, costing up to 85,000 points per night. 25 annual elite night credits: Give yourself a boost toward higher levels of elite status each year. IHG One Rewards Premier Credit Card The IHG Premier card can help you earn more IHG points and enhance your stays. Highlights include: Earn points: 10x points on IHG stays, 5x points on travel, at gas stations, and restaurants, and 3x points on all other purchases. Automatic IHG Platinum Elite status : Includes perks like 60% bonus points, priority check-in and extended checkout (based on availability), complimentary room upgrades (based on availability), a welcome amenity, and more. Anniversary free night certificate: Redeemable at any IHG hotel in the portfolio, costing up to 40,000 points per night. Fourth night free on award stays: Redeem points for 3 consecutive nights and get the fourth night free. Hilton Honors American Express Aspire Card For those looking to maximize the value of their Hilton stays, there is no better card option than the  Hilton Aspire card . Benefits include: Earn points: 14x points on Hilton purchases, 7x points on flights, car rentals, and U.S. restaurants, and 3x points on all other purchases. Complimentary Hilton Diamond status : Includes 100% bonus points on stays, complimentary room upgrades (upon availability), complimentary breakfast, Executive Lounge access, and more. Anniversary fee night award: Redeemable at nearly any Hilton hotel worldwide, plus the ability to earn a second and third free night award with $30,000 in annual spend, and $60,000 in annual spend, respectively. $400 annual Hilton resort credit: Receive annual statement credits for eligible purchases made with participating Hilton Resorts (up to $200 in statement credits semiannually). Other premium credit cards, like the Platinum Card® from American Express , include elite status with brands such as Marriott and Hilton. Hot Tip: Cards that earn transferable rewards can be a good option for booking stays at these properties. You can transfer Amex Membership Rewards to Marriott and Hilton, Chase Ultimate Rewards to Marriott and IHG, and Bilt Rewards to all 3 programs, making select Amex , Chase , and Bilt cards worth considering. Best Michelin Key Hotels With Hilton Points Roku Kyoto, LXR Hotels & Resorts (Kyoto, Japan): 1 Key If you want to experience a quieter side of Kyoto, Roku Kyoto, LXR Hotels & Resorts offers a luxury retreat unlike the city’s more centrally located hotels. Nestled in the foothills of northern Kyoto near the historic Takagamine district, the property feels more like a secluded resort than an urban hotel, surrounded by mountains, gardens, and the tranquil waters of the Tenjin River. The Roku Kyoto, LXR Hotels & Resorts, offers a serene escape from the crowds at Kyoto’s popular sights. Image Credit: Jarrod West The property blends contemporary luxury with traditional Japanese aesthetics , featuring natural materials, floor-to-ceiling windows, and views of surrounding mountains. Select accommodations offer private onsen-style baths, while the overall design centers around nature and Kyoto’s centuries-old cultural heritage. Average cash price: $600 to $1,200 Award price: From 140,000 Hilton points per night » Related: 6 Things To Know About Hilton’s ROKU KYOTO, LXR Hotels & Resorts [Review] Flemings Mayfair (London, United Kingdom): 1 Key Flemings Mayfair is a boutique hotel with historic charm in one of London’s best neighborhoods, offering an alternative to London’s larger luxury hotels. Located in the heart of Mayfair, the property features 13 Georgian townhouses dating back to the 18th century and has welcomed guests since 1851, making it one of London’s oldest hotels. The Flemings Mayfair is perfectly located for a visit to London. Image Credit: Jarrod West The location is one of the hotel’s greatest strengths. Green Park, Buckingham Palace, Bond Street shopping, and many of London’s top restaurants and galleries are all within walking distance, allowing guests to experience the best of the city while staying in a more residential setting. Average cash price: $400 to $800 Award price: From 105,000 Hilton points per night Best Michelin Key Hotels With IHG Points InterContinental Phuket Resort (Phuket, Thailand): 2 Keys If you’re looking for a luxurious beachfront retreat that combines tropical scenery, exceptional dining, and resort-style amenities, the InterContinental Phuket Resort is one of the best options on the island. Relax your cares away at the InterContinental Phuket Resort. Image Credit: IHG Situated on the white sands of Kamala Beach and framed by jungle-covered hills and the Andaman Sea, the resort offers a peaceful escape while staying within reach of Phuket’s attractions. If you’re willing to visit in the low season, you can score excellent deals with both cash and award nights. Average cash price: $300 to $900 Award price: From 40,000 to 120,000 IHG points per night InterContinental Yokohama Pier 8 (Yokohama, Japan): 1 Key The InterContinental Yokohama Pier 8 combines the energy of a vibrant city with the relaxed vibe of a seaside resort. Located in Yokohama’s Minato Mirai district and surrounded by the sea, the hotel offers a tranquil setting while remaining close to the city’s top attractions , including Yokohama Chinatown, the Cup Noodles Museum, and the iconic Cosmo Clock Ferris wheel. Use your points for a room with a beautiful water view at the InterContinental Yokohama Pier 8. Image Credit: IHG The rooms are inspired by the harborfront, with soft maritime tones, natural materials, and expansive windows that frame views of Yokohama Bay, the city skyline, and passing cruise ships. The property’s waterfront terraces and Japanese gardens make it feel more like a destination resort than a city hotel , blending Japanese hospitality and contemporary design. With award pricing as low as 39,000 points per night (though nights at this rate are scarce), this property could provide great value for a free night certificate from one of the IHG co-branded cards. Average cash price: $300 to $700 Award price: From 39,000 to 100,000 IHG points per night Best Michelin Key Hotels With Marriott Points Phulay Bay, a Ritz-Carlton Reserve (Krabi, Thailand): 3 Keys The Phulay Bay, a Ritz-Carlton Reserve is one of Thailand’s most exclusive resort experiences. Set along the Andaman Sea against a backdrop of limestone karsts and tropical gardens, the resort was the very first property in the Ritz-Carlton Reserve collection and remains one of its most celebrated. Take in the views at the Phulay Bay, a Ritz-Carlton Reserve. Image Credit: Marriott With its exceptional service, villa-style accommodations, and breathtaking views, Phulay Bay is particularly well-suited for honeymooners, milestone celebrations, and travelers seeking a more intimate alternative to Thailand’s larger beach resorts. Average cash price: $800 to $2,500 Award price: From 106,000 to 158,000 Marriott points per night Hotel The Mitsui Kyoto, a Luxury Collection Hotel & Spa (Kyoto, Japan): 3 Keys The Mitsui Kyoto, a Luxury Collection Hotel & Spa seamlessly blends Kyoto’s rich heritage with contemporary sophistication. Located directly across from the UNESCO World Heritage site Nijo Castle, the hotel occupies the former estate of the Mitsui family, and welcomes guests through a 300-year-old gate into its hidden sanctuary. Enjoy a luxury stay at Hotel The Mitsui Kyoto, a Luxury Collection Hotel & Spa. Image Credit: Marriott The hotel’s rooms and suites give a traditional Japanese tea house a modern twist, featuring natural materials, artisanal details, and deep soaking tubs inspired by Kyoto’s seasons. Many accommodations overlook the hotel’s courtyard gardens or Nijo Castle. Some suites elevate the experience with private outdoor onsen baths fed by natural thermal springs from more than 3,000 feet beneath the hotel. Average cash price: $900 to $1,800 Award price: From 112,000 to 168,000 Marriott points per night Final Thoughts A Michelin Key hotel doesn’t have to come with a large price tag. By redeeming Hilton, IHG, and Marriott points, you can experience some of the world’s top hotels while saving thousands of dollars in the process. If you have elite status from your credit card, you can also enjoy perks like room upgrades, complimentary breakfast, on-site credits, late checkout, and more. It pays to use points for your stay, leaving your hard-earned dollars for other experiences during your travels.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://genk.vn/rss/home.rss\",\"feedTitle\":\"Genk.vn\",\"feedIcon\":\"http