&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"27-year-old locksmith spent $7,000 on tools, works around the clock in NYC: 'I'd never held a screwdriver in my life' before starting\",\"link\":\"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/18/how-27-year-old-left-her-job-to-start-locksmith-girl-of-nyc.html\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cnbc.com%2F2026%2F07%2F18%2Fhow-27-year-old-left-her-job-to-start-locksmith-girl-of-nyc.html&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dcnbc.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T13:05:01.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Yona Sahar, founder of Locksmith Girl of NYC, spent two years learning the trade. Now, she runs her own business while working around the clock.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://feeds.bloomberg.com/economics/news.rss\",\"feedTitle\":\"Bloomberg Economics\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=feeds.bloomberg.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"Chinese Trucks Pose New Peril in Mexico’s US Tariff Relief Bid\",\"link\":\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-18/chinese-trucks-pose-new-peril-in-mexico-s-us-tariff-relief-bid\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bloomberg.com%2Fnews%2Farticles%2F2026-07-18%2Fchinese-trucks-pose-new-peril-in-mexico-s-us-tariff-relief-bid&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dfeeds.bloomberg.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T13:00:02.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Mexico’s growing fleet of Chinese-made heavy trucks is becoming a new obstacle in its quest for relief from US tariffs, as Washington presses for assurances that its southern neighbor isn’t becoming a backdoor for Chinese manufacturers to enter North America.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://search.cnbc.com/rs/search/combinedcms/view.xml?partnerId=wrss01&id=100727362\",\"feedTitle\":\"International: Top News And Analysis\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=cnbc.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"I left the U.S. for Trinidad and Tobago: My family of 4 spends about $3,000 a month—and we've never been happier\",\"link\":\"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/18/i-left-the-us-for-trinidad-and-tobago-my-family-of-4-spends-3k-a-month-weve-never-been-happier.html\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cnbc.com%2F2026%2F07%2F18%2Fi-left-the-us-for-trinidad-and-tobago-my-family-of-4-spends-3k-a-month-weve-never-been-happier.html&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dcnbc.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T13:00:01.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Chantel Henry lives with her husband and two kids in Trinidad. She says living there has made her American Dream more affordable.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"Is the Amex Platinum Card Worth It? An $895 Breakdown [2026]\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/credit-cards/reviews/american-express-platinum-card/worth-it/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/02/Amex-Platinum-Card-In-Hand-Upgraded-Points-LLC-2.jpeg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T13:00:00.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Is the Platinum Card® from American Express worth its $895 annual fee ( rates & fees )? For travelers who use the card’s lounge access and extensive statement credits, it’s worth it, but harder to justify if you won’t use the card’s benefits. The break-even point is roughly $895 of the card’s more than $3,700 in annual credits and benefits. Quick Overview: Is the Amex Platinum Card Worth It? The Amex Platinum card is worth it if you’d use at least $895 of the card’s more than $3,700 in annual value, which includes more than $3,000 in statement credits . That’s typically achievable for frequent travelers who use the lounge access and credits, but it’s not worth it if you don’t use enough of the card’s benefits to get at least $895 in value each year. A frequent traveler may realistically get $1,500 to $2,000 in value each year, or about $605 to $1,105 after the $895 annual fee (Source: American Express ). Several credits require enrollment and expire if you don’t use them, so the value you get from them depends on your spending patterns. How the Amex Platinum Card Credits Stack up Against the $895 Fee The Amex Platinum card’s annual credits add up to more than $3,000, but you don’t need to use all of them to offset the $895 annual fee — and you should only count a credit at full value when it replaces spending you would have made anyway. Monthly, quarterly, and semiannual restrictions may limit the value you can get from the credits (Source: American Express ). SCROLL FOR MORE Credit Annual Amount Cadence Enrollment Required Key Condition(s) Airline incidental fee credit Up to $200 Annual Yes Incidental fees, not airfare; qualifying airline selection required CLEAR+ membership fee credit Up to $219 Annual No Membership automatically renews Digital entertainment credit Up to $300 Monthly Yes Direct purchases from participating providers Equinox+ credit Up to $300 Annual Yes Membership or digital subscription lululemon credit Up to $300 Quarterly Yes Eligible U.S. stores and online purchases Oura ring credit Up to $200 Annual Yes Direct purchase with Oura; does not apply to membership fee Prepaid hotel credit Up to $600 Semiannual No Eligible prepaid booking with Fine Hotels + Resorts or The Hotel Collection (the latter has a 2-night minimum) Resy credit Up to $400 Quarterly Yes Eligible purchases at qualifying Resy restaurants SoulCycle bike credit Up to $300 Annual Yes Must purchase through the bike offer portal Uber Cash Up to $200 Monthly Yes U.S. rides and Uber Eats orders; must add card to Uber account and pay with an Amex card Uber One credit Up to $120 Annual Yes Automatically renewing U.S. membership Walmart+ membership credit Up to $155 Monthly Yes Select a monthly plan; annual memberships not covered Total Annual Credit Value Up to $3,294 The up-to- $120 Global Entry or up-to-$85 TSA PreCheck credit is available every 4 years — about $30 or $21 per year. A frequent traveler might use these benefits to get more than $2,000 in annual value: Airline fee credit: $200 Digital entertainment credit: $300 CLEAR+ membership fee: $219 Prepaid hotel credit: $600 Resy credit: $400 Uber One and Uber Cash credits: $320 With a credit value of $2,039, you’d come out ahead by $1,144 after the $895 annual fee. That’s before counting the value of airport lounge access , elite status , travel protections , and rewards. » Related: 26 Benefits and Perks of the Amex Platinum Card ($7,500 or More in Value) Who the Amex Platinum Card Is (And Isn’t) Worth It For Worth It if You Travel Often If you take at least 5 trips each year, naturally use at least $1,500 in credits , and often visit airport lounges and use hotel and rental car elite status, the Amex Platinum card has clear value. You can get more than $600 in value beyond the annual fee from statement credits, plus the value of lounge visits and elite status. Visits to airport lounges can add value to the Amex Platinum card. Image Credit: Ryan Smith Borderline if You Travel Occasionally The value of the Amex Platinum card for occasional travelers depends on how much you can use the credits. Taking around 2 to 5 trips annually with about $1,000 in statement credits use can put you $105 ahead, though occasional lounge visits and hotel benefits could add value. Not Worth It if You Rarely Travel The Amex Platinum card offers lifestyle benefits, but is probably not worth it if you take fewer than 2 trips annually and use less than $700 of the statement credits . After the annual fee, you’d be behind by at least $195, and lounge access, elite status, and travel protections don’t offer much value without regular travel. The Catch: Use-It-or-Lose-It Credits Many of the Amex Platinum card’s credits are applied monthly, quarterly, or semiannually, and unused portions typically expire rather than roll over . Realistically, you may not use all of the benefits in each credit period, so you might only get $400 to $900 worth of credit value as a light user, despite having much more value available. Spending money just to use up a credit doesn’t give you the full face value. The real value is in using statement credits that replace spending you would have made anyway, such as using the airline fee credit to upgrade seats or check bags, or spending the Resy credit at a favorite restaurant. » Related: Why You Don’t Have To Use Every Amex Platinum Credit To Find Lasting Value If It’s Not Worth It: Downgrades and Alternatives If you won’t get enough value from the Amex Platinum card to justify its annual fee, a lower-fee card may better suit your needs. The American Express® Gold Card has a $325 annual fee with fewer statement credits but more everyday rewards ( rates & fees ). Downgrading from the Amex Platinum card to the Amex Gold card can preserve Membership Rewards points. The Chase Sapphire Preferred® Card earns Chase Ultimate Rewards points instead of Membership Rewards, but it offers travel rewards and benefits — including a $100 annual hotel credit and credit for TSA PreCheck or Global Entry — for a modest $95 annual fee. Hot Tip: See how these cards stack up in our Amex Platinum card versus Amex Gold card and Chase Sapphire Preferred card versus Amex Platinum card comparisons. Final Thoughts The Amex Platinum card ‘s value depends on which credits you use each year, so the real test is to calculate the credits you expect to use and compare it to the card’s $895 annual fee. Refer to the statement credit table above to compare the credit value. The card is worth it when your total exceeds the annual fee, not because it has strong theoretical value.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://feeds.bloomberg.com/economics/news.rss\",\"feedTitle\":\"Bloomberg Economics\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=feeds.bloomberg.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"Warsh Shows His Inner Hawk as Inflation Debate Heats Up\",\"link\":\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-18/warsh-shows-his-inner-hawk-as-inflation-debate-heats-up\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bloomberg.com%2Fnews%2Farticles%2F2026-07-18%2Fwarsh-shows-his-inner-hawk-as-inflation-debate-heats-up&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dfeeds.bloomberg.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T13:00:00.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"Federal Reserve Chairman Kevin Warsh hammered home the message this week that the US central bank is on inflation watch. The test to deliver on that promise could come soon.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://feeds.bloomberg.com/economics/news.rss\",\"feedTitle\":\"Bloomberg Economics\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=feeds.bloomberg.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"Kerala Aims to Attract $1 Billion In Shipbuilding From Investors\",\"link\":\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-18/kerala-aims-to-attract-1-billion-in-shipbuilding-from-investors\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bloomberg.com%2Fnews%2Farticles%2F2026-07-18%2Fkerala-aims-to-attract-1-billion-in-shipbuilding-from-investors&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dfeeds.bloomberg.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-18T12:31:44.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"The Kerala Government on Saturday clarified that the southern Indian state aims to attract 100 billion rupees ($1 billion) of investment in shipbuilding from multiple investors, rather than a single source, in the coming years.\"