ogle.com/s2/favicons?domain=cnbc.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Business\",\"title\":\"Trump rips Canada as wildfire smoke spreads, says U.S. will add pollution cost to tariffs\",\"link\":\"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/17/trump-canada-wildfire-tariffs.html\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cnbc.com%2F2026%2F07%2F17%2Ftrump-canada-wildfire-tariffs.html&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dcnbc.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-17T19:40:55.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"The smoke coming from active wildfires in Canada has led to unhealthy air quality in swaths of the U.S., which could affect the World Cup final in New Jersey.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://www.theverge.com/rss/index.xml\",\"feedTitle\":\"The Verge\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.theverge.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Tech\",\"title\":\"TikTok is testing an AI likeness detection tool\",\"link\":\"https://www.theverge.com/tech/967486/tiktok-ai-likeness-detection-tool\",\"image\":\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/23951408/STK051_VRG_Illo_N_Barclay_4_tiktok.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-17T19:34:30.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"TikTok is starting to test an opt-in tool that scans for AI likenesses and lets creators report them to the company, as spotted by social media consultant Matt Navarra . The tool is initially being tested with \\\"some\\\" US creators, TikTok US spokesperson Zachary Kizer tells The Verge . YouTube has been working on a similar tool and recently made it available to all adult users . Creators who are part of TikTok's test and want to use the tool will first have to verify their identity with a company called Jumio . You'll have to do a real-time selfie scan and an ID check, but Kizer says that \\\"TikTok does not retain ID documents, and facial informati … Read the full story at The Verge.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://www.investing.com/rss/302.rss\",\"feedTitle\":\"Cryptocurrency Opinion & Analysis\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.investing.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Investing\",\"title\":\"Bitcoin’s CPI Rally Is Fake, Built on Borrowed Money\",\"link\":\"https://www.investing.com/analysis/bitcoins-cpi-rally-is-fake-built-on-borrowed-money-200684162\",\"image\":\"/api/og-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.investing.com%2Fanalysis%2Fbitcoins-cpi-rally-is-fake-built-on-borrowed-money-200684162&rss=&icon=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.google.com%2Fs2%2Ffavicons%3Fdomain%3Dwww.investing.com%26sz%3D64\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-17T19:28:00.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/feed/\",\"feedTitle\":\"UpgradedPoints.com\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=upgradedpoints.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Others\",\"title\":\"Choice Privileges Raises Award Prices in Japan Again\",\"link\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/news/choice-privileges-second-devaluation-japan/\",\"image\":\"https://upgradedpoints.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/02/Ice-Hotel-Sweden-Choice-Ascend-Warm-Room.jpg\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-17T19:19:01.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"For the second time in the last few weeks, Choice Privileges has significantly increased the points required for award redemptions at many hotels across Japan. Though the program has undergone several major devaluations in recent years, this one may have just pushed away Choice loyalists who were still upset from the last devaluation. Here’s a look at the latest change with award redemption rates across Choice Privileges hotels across Japan and what it means for Choice Privileges members going forward. Choice Privileges Further Increases Points Pricing at Many Hotels Across Japan Choice Privileges , the loyalty program of  Choice Hotels , was long considered an underrated program, thanks to its solid award rates and its massive hotel footprint , which includes thousands of properties worldwide. Unfortunately, the program underwent a major devaluation last month, during which many properties in major Japanese cities saw  point requirements jump by as much as 200%.  Now, Choice Privileges has once again increased the number of points required for award stays at many of its hotels in Japan — and done so without any advance warning, just like last time. Certain hotels in cities like Tokyo, Osaka, and Fukuoka now require as many as 30,000 points per night. Choice Hotels award nights now cost a lot more points than they did a few months ago. Image Credit: Choice Hotels For example, nearly every property across Tokyo now costs more than 20,000 Choice points per night — up to 30,000 points, depending on the location. Though the program once provided solid value at properties, particularly when cash rates were high, the award rates are now much higher than travelers are used to. » Related: The 23 Best Ways To Redeem Choice Privileges Points [2026] What This Means for Choice Privileges Members Simply put, this is another major devaluation that further diminishes the value of Choice Privileges points . At Upgraded Points, we  value Choice Privileges points  at 0.6 cents apiece. However, this valuation will likely drop as Japan has long been seen as one of the strongest redemption sweet spots in the Choice Privileges program. As someone who is heading to Japan for the 10th time in the coming weeks, this devaluation really hurts. While Choice Hotels is better known in North America for its budget brands, its Japanese portfolio has always been interesting. This is mostly due to centrally located business hotels that could be booked for remarkably low award rates despite expensive cash prices In the past, Choice Privileges has frequently offered bonus points deals and  point-purchasing promotions , which have provided excellent value when redeemed for award stays. However, with award prices now significantly higher, purchasing points specifically for award redemptions may no longer be worthwhile. Hot Tip: A 200% increase hurts, and it’s customer-unfriendly. However, it’s worth noting that Marriott Bonvoy and Hilton Honors regularly charge 6 digits to book a hotel in Japan with points. Final Thoughts Again, and without warning, Choice Privileges has increased the number of points required for award redemptions at many of its hotels across Japan. This is the second major devaluation in recent weeks affecting what used to be one of the program’s best sweet spots. For those with a healthy stash of Choice points (or any points for that matter), this is a reminder to use them sooner rather than later. Devaluations are an unfortunate fact of life.\",\"replyCount\":0,\"viewCount\":0},{\"feedUrl\":\"https://www.theverge.com/rss/index.xml\",\"feedTitle\":\"The Verge\",\"feedIcon\":\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=www.theverge.com&sz=64\",\"feedCategory\":\"Tech\",\"title\":\"Pebble founder Eric Migicovsky says his 30-day warranty is all about trust\",\"link\":\"https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/967471/pebble-smartwatch-warranty-repairs-migicovsky-interview\",\"image\":\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/11/258119_Pebble_Core_2_Duo_Review_AKrales_0093.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\",\"pubDate\":\"2026-07-17T19:14:42.000Z\",\"createDate\":null,\"content\":\"The Pebble Appstore is filled with stuff, but you don’t need much of it. | Photo by Amelia Holowaty Krales / The Verge Pebble founder Eric Migicovsky says buyers of its new e-paper smartwatches should know what they're signing up for and trust Pebble to make things right if they run into issues, despite the short warranty. \\\"I think the most important thing is trust,\\\" Migicovsky told me in an interview this week. \\\"Do people trust the product that we're making and